<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hird</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hirð, De Håndgangne Menn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The hird was, in Scandinavian history, at first an informal retinue of armed companions, the hirdmen or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/housecarl">housecarls</a>, and later the core of the royal army and a formal royal court household. The word comes from Old Norse hirð, from Old English hir(e)d, 'household, retinue', or perhaps a German cognate. By the reign of Håkon IV (1204–1263) the Norwegian hird had subdivisions on continental patterns, squires, men-at-arms and knights, its law code, the Hirdskraa, was read when the whole hird assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>, and its upper ranks supplied royal officials. Under Håkon V (1299–1319) its Norse titles gave way to continental ones, and the king was technically first among equals.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Although the word appears often in the Norse sagas and law codes, hird is a medieval term: the sagas were mostly written down in the 12th century, in the language of their own day. It is therefore uncertain what word it replaced in earlier times. Danish sources use hlid or lið for the warrior following of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Canute the Great</a>. In Old Norse hirð could also mean herd, and the word has been linked to the idea of a hearthguard, the men of a lord's own home and hearth.</p><p>A member of a hird was a hirdman, a word still used in Norwegian and Swedish. The title first described the informal companions or retainers of the powerful, above all their companions in arms in the unruly and often still pagan earlier times, and it appears even in Norse mythology. Later it came to mean something closer to a courtier, a development not unlike that of the thegn or the Roman comes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In the 13th-century Norwegian court the hird had several grades. The squires were the kertilsveinr, literally candle-men, who were required to hold candles at the hird's ceremonies; the men-at-arms were the hirdmenn; and the knights were the skutilsveinr, literally table-men. In addition there were the gestir, who received only half pay and served as a kind of intelligence service. They were not allowed to sit at the king's table for supper except on Christmas Day and Easter Day.</p><p>Once the royal hird had become a formal court, hirdman was the title of the highest of its four ranks: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> who were allowed to sit in the royal council, the nearest feudal equivalent of a cabinet, and so had a say in government. Most officials serving away from the court were also enrolled in the hird. During the reign of King Magnus VI the older laws of the hird were brought into the Hirdskraa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hird” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Norway the law code of the <b>hird</b>, the royal household, was read when the whole hird assembled at Christmas and Easter.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
